\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}

\taxon{Theorem}
\title{The two top exterior words are the type-D fork highest vectors}

\p{Continue with the Cartan and Borel of \ref{fcap-002X}. For the
increasing exterior basis #{u_I}, set

##{\Omega=u_{\{1,\ldots,\ell\}},\qquad
\widehat\Omega=u_{\{1,\ldots,\ell-1\}}.}

Every positive chain operator kills #{\Omega} by duplicate creation. It
kills #{\widehat\Omega} either by the same mechanism or, on the final chain
edge, because the contracted last coordinate is absent. The positive fork
operator kills #{\Omega} by duplicate creation and #{\widehat\Omega}
because coordinate #{\ell-1} is already present. The preceding
root-space generation therefore makes both vectors highest for
#{\mathfrak b}.}

\p{Since #{H_i} has eigenvalue #{+\frac12} on an occupied coordinate and
#{-\frac12} on an unoccupied one, their weights are

##{\operatorname{wt}(\Omega)
=\omega_\ell=\tfrac12(\epsilon_1+\cdots+\epsilon_\ell),}

##{\operatorname{wt}(\widehat\Omega)
=\omega_{\ell-1}
=\tfrac12(\epsilon_1+\cdots+\epsilon_{\ell-1}-\epsilon_\ell).}

The first word lies in #{S^+=\bigwedge^{\mathrm{even}}W} exactly when
#{\ell} is even, while the second has the opposite parity. Hence, with
#{S^\pm} defined by exterior parity as in \ref{fcap-0025},

##{\begin{array}{c|cc}
&S^+&S^-\\ \hline
\ell\text{ even}&L(\omega_\ell)&L(\omega_{\ell-1})\\
\ell\text{ odd}&L(\omega_{\ell-1})&L(\omega_\ell)
\end{array}}

The parity label is therefore not an independent name for the all-plus
weight.}

\p{For completeness, each displayed module is irreducible for the type-D
Lie action. The commuting #{H_i} have distinct joint eigenvalues on the
one-dimensional lines #{\mathbb C u_I}, so polynomial projections in the
#{H_i} extract a nonzero basis word from any nonzero invariant subspace.
The chain operators move occupied coordinates, while the fork pair creates
or removes two of them. Their occupancy graph connects all subsets of a
fixed parity. The invariant subspace consequently contains every basis word
of that parity. This proves irreducibility without identifying it with the
separate group-level theorem.}

\p{The exact formal comparison is presently rational. In the standard
model set #{S_{\mathbb Q}=\bigwedge_{\mathbb Q}\mathbb Q^\ell}. The basis
map

##{\begin{aligned}
\mathbb C\otimes_{\mathbb Q}S_{\mathbb Q}
&\longrightarrow\bigwedge_{\mathbb C}\mathbb C^\ell,\\
z\otimes u_I&\longmapsto z u_I.
\end{aligned}}

is an equivalence. Every #{E_i} and #{F_i} above has matrix entries
#{0,1,-1}; the coroot matrices are integral and the #{H_i} matrices are
half-integral. Thus these actions, their annihilation identities, and their
weights commute with extension from #{\mathbb Q} to #{\mathbb C}. A chosen
complex polar-dual basis then transports the standard calculation to
#{W\oplus W'}. TauCeti proves the two simple-generator annihilation
statements and exact fork weights over #{\mathbb Q}
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.SpinPolarizationData.typeDSpinRep_serreE_exteriorBasis_univ_eq_zero}
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.SpinPolarizationData.typeDSpinRep_serreE_exteriorBasis_univ_erase_last_eq_zero}
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.SpinPolarizationData.isCartanWeightVector_typeDSpinRep_exteriorBasis_univ}
\leanref/tauceti{TauCeti.SpinPolarizationData.isCartanWeightVector_typeDSpinRep_exteriorBasis_univ_erase_last}.
The displayed scalar-extension and Borel steps are the mathematical bridge
to the complex highest-weight conclusion, not an asserted packaged Lean
base-change theorem.}

\p{\citet{Proposition 20.15 and proof, pp. 305--306}{fulton2004representation}
independently identify the two irreducible half-spin modules and give the
same parity-dependent fork assignment after the polar-dual rescaling already
recorded in \ref{fcap-0025}. Their occupied Cartan sign is positive, as it
is here.}

\p{\citet{Proposition 6.2.3, pp. 314--315}{goodman2009symmetry}
independently gives the two irreducible modules and their signed half-sum
weights. Its creation space is identified with our #{W}, but its occupied
Cartan sign is negative. Under
#{H_i^{\mathrm{Tau}}=-H_i^{\mathrm{GW}}} the weights and positive systems
both reverse. The exterior-parity labels remain fixed; the assignment of
the two fork highest weights to them must be translated with the Cartan sign
and Borel.}
